Boxing and unboxing are essential concepts in C# that allow conversion between value and reference types. Boxing occurs when a value type is converted to a reference type, such as when an `int` is assigned to an `object`. Unboxing is the reverse process, where a reference type is converted back to a value type. However, boxing and unboxing incur performance costs, as they involve creating and destroying objects on the heap. To minimize these costs, it is essential to understand when boxing and unboxing occur and how to avoid them when possible.

