Type parameterization is a technique that allows us to define a class, method, or interface that can work with multiple data types. This is achieved by introducing type parameters, which are placeholders for specific types that will be specified when the class or method is instantiated. Type parameters are defined using the `where` keyword, followed by a constraint that specifies the characteristics of the type that can be used.

For example, consider a simple generic class `Box<T>` that can store a value of any type `T`. We can define this class as follows:
```csharp
public class Box<T>
{
