    public Box(T value)
    {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public T GetValue()
    {
        return value;
    }
}
```
In this example, `T` is a type parameter that can be replaced with any type, such as `int`, `string`, or `CustomClass`. The `Box<T>` class is type-safe, meaning that it will only work with the specified type `T`, and will not allow any other type to be used.

We can also apply constraints to type parameters to ensure that they meet certain criteria. For example, we can constrain `T` to be a reference type using the `class` constraint:
```csharp
public class Box<T> where T : class
{
    // ...
}
```
This constraint ensures that `T` can only be a reference type, such as a class or an interface.

Generic methods are another important application of type parameterization. A generic method is a method that can work with multiple data types, just like a generic class. We can define a generic method using the `where` keyword, followed by a constraint that specifies the characteristics of the type that can be used.

For example, consider a generic method `Swap<T>` that swaps the values of two variables of type `T`:
```csharp
public static void Swap<T>(ref T a, ref T b)
{
